  "literature_map": "Luedtke and van der Laan (2016, Theorem 1 and Sections 5--7) characterize pathwise differentiability of optimal value and construct online one-step inference at fixed complexity; exact ties remain the source of nonregularity rather than a finite-sample minimax analysis. Whitehouse, Chen, Austern, and Syrgkanis (2025, Theorem 1 and the conclusion) smooth the same maximum with a softmax orthogonal score and obtain root-\\(n\\) asymptotic linearity under a gap-density condition and nuisance rates; their paper explicitly leaves data-adaptive smoothing and irregular efficiency open. Nguyen, Barrett, and Jun (2025, Algorithm 1 and Theorem 3) construct HAVER by a lower-confidence pivot and head averaging and prove instance-dependent MSE upper bounds for passively sampled arm means; the theorem has no random patient groups, no causal logging layer, and no global all-estimator converse. Jiao, Venkat, Han, and Weissman (2015, Sections I--II and the estimator/lower-bound sections) split smooth from nonsmooth cells, estimate polynomial surrogates by factorial moments, and pair the construction with fuzzy hypotheses; their proved rates cover additive univariate probability functionals, not a multivariate ratio maximum. Wu and Yang (2016, Theorem 1 and Sections IV--V) make approximation--moment duality determine both the entropy estimator and its lower mixture, including the consistency threshold; the destination here first changes at the multivariate polynomial and multinomial covariance step. Cai and Low (2011, Theorems 1 and 5) provide the closest normal-means template for a kinked functional, but their coordinatewise absolute-value geometry does not control a growing simplex of tie faces. Spalek (2008, Theorem 7) gives an explicit dual polynomial proving the square-root approximate-degree obstruction for Boolean OR; the Boolean restriction of the many-arm maximum therefore rules out any dimension-free low-degree certificate, although approximate degree alone is not a statistical lower bound. Luedtke and van der Laan (2018, Theorems 1--2) treat maxima with growing index sets by stabilized one-step methods and asymptotic coverage under growth and remainder conditions, not sparse multinomial cells. Zhou, Athey, and Wager (2023, Theorem 2) obtain policy-regret bounds over restricted policy classes; their decision loss is distinct from MSE for the unrestricted oracle value. The searches for discrete multiclass Bayes-accuracy estimation, grouped maximum-mean minimax risk, and polynomial estimation of a sum of conditional maxima found classification-error estimation and generic functional-estimation papers, but no result occupying the exact unknown-mass, unknown-logging, jointly growing \\(d,K\\) target--method pair.",
